"Universality for SLE(4) (https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1356)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.0 years ago; Title: Universality for SLE(4)\nTitle: Universality for SLE(4)\nAuthors: Jason Miller\nDate: Thu Oct  7 07:21:38 2010\n\nWe resolve a conjecture of Sheffield that $\\SLE(4)$, a conformally invariant random curve, is the universal limit of the chordal zero-height contours of random surfaces with isotropic, uniformly convex potentials. Specifically, we study the \\emph{Ginzburg-Landau $\\nabla \\phi$ interface model} or \\emph{anharmonic crystal} on $D_n = D \\cap \\tfrac{1}{n} \\Z^2$ for $D \\subseteq \\C$ a bounded, simply connected Jordan domain with smooth boundary. This is the massless field with Hamiltonian $\\CH(h) = \\sum_{x \\sim y} \\CV(h(x) - h(y))$ with $\\CV$ symmetric and uniformly convex and $h(x) = \\phi(x)$ for $x \\in \\partial D_n$, $\\phi \\colon \\partial D_n \\to \\R$ a given function. We show that the macroscopic chordal contours of $h$ are asymptotically described by $\\SLE(4)$ for appropriately chosen $\\phi$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation (https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.12256)\nciteturn4academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.3 years ago; In this paper, we introduce and study the annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation, which is a continuous and finite point process in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose definition is mostly inspired by the spectral sample of Bernoulli percolation introduced in [GPS10] by Garban, Pete and Schramm.\nTitle: The annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation\nAuthors: Hugo Vanneuville\nDate: Fri Jun 28 15:01:23 2019\n\nIn this paper, we introduce and study the annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation, which is a continuous and finite point process in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose definition is mostly inspired by the spectral sample of Bernoulli percolation introduced in [GPS10] by Garban, Pete and Schramm. We show a clustering effect as well as estimates on the full lower tail of this spectral object. Our main motivation is the study of two models of dynamical critical Voronoi percolation in the plane. In the first model, the Voronoi tiling does not evolve in time while the colors of the cells are resampled at rate $1$. In the second model, the centers of the cells move according to (independent) long range stable L\\'evy processes but the colors do not evolve in time. We prove that for these two dynamical processes there exist almost surely exceptional times with an unbounded monochromatic component.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectra of Poisson functionals and applications in continuum percolation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.13502)\nciteturn4academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Title: Spectra of Poisson functionals and applications in continuum percolation ... Let $\\eta$ be a Poisson random measure (defined on some Polish space), and let $F(\\eta)$ be a square-integrable functional of $\\eta$.\nTitle: Spectra of Poisson functionals and applications in continuum percolation\nAuthors: Chinmoy Bhattacharjee, Giovanni Peccati, D. Yogeshwaran\nDate: Thu Jul 18 13:33:57 2024\n\nLet $\\eta$ be a Poisson random measure (defined on some Polish space), and let $F(\\eta)$ be a square-integrable functional of $\\eta$. In this paper we define and study a new notion of {\\it spectral point process} associated with $F(\\eta)$, and use such an object to study sharp noise instability and sensitivity properties of planar critical continuum percolation models under spatial birth-death (Ornstein-Uhlenbeck) dynamics -- the notion of sharp noise instability being a natural strengthening of the absence of noise stability. The concept of spectral point process is defined by exploiting the Wiener-It\\^o chaos expansion of $F$, and represents a natural continuum counterpart to the notion of {\\it spectral sample}, as introduced in Garban, Pete and Schramm (2010), in the context of discrete percolation models. In the particular case where $\\eta$ is a marked Poisson measure, we use Hoeffding-ANOVA decompositions to establish an explicit connection with the notion of {\\it annealed spectral sample}, introduced in Vanneuville (2021) in the context of Poisson-Voronoi percolation. We also relate spectral processes with an appropriate notion of {\\it pivotal processes}. As applications, we show sharp noise instability of crossing events in the critical Poisson Boolean model with unit-radius balls and, using an observation of Vanneuville, we obtain sharp noise sensitivity (as well as sharp noise instability) for crossing events in the Poisson Voronoi percolation model. As an important ingredient, we prove quasi-multiplicativity of the $4$-arm probabilities in the critical Poisson Boolean percolation model.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectra of Poisson functionals and applications in continuum percolation | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2407.13502v1)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; @misc{bhattacharjee2024spectrapoissonfunctionalsapplications, title={Spectra of Poisson functionals and applications in continuum percolation}, author={Chinmoy Bhattacharjee and Giovanni Peccati and D.Yogeshwaran}, year={2024}, eprint={2407.13502}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.PR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.13502}, }\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{bhattacharjee2024spectrapoissonfunctionalsapplications, title={Spectra of Poisson functionals and applications in continuum percolation}, author={Chinmoy Bhattacharjee and Giovanni Peccati and D. Yogeshwaran}, year={2024}, eprint={2407.13502}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.PR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.13502}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334129603_The_annealed_spectral_sample_of_Voronoi_percolation)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # The annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1906.12256 ... In this paper, we study the annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation, which is a continuous and finite point process in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose definition is mostly inspired by the spectral sample of Bernoulli percolation introduced in [GPS10] by Garban, Pete and Schramm. ... To read the file of this research,\nPreprint\n\n# The annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation\n\n  * June 2019\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1906.12256\n\nAuthors:\n\nHugo Vanneuville\n\nHugo Vanneuville\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we study the annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation, which is a continuous and finite point process in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose definition is mostly inspired by the spectral sample of Bernoulli percolation introduced in [GPS10] by Garban, Pete and Schramm. We show a clustering effect as well as a sharp estimate on the full lower tail of this spectral object. Our main motivation is the study of two models of dynamical critical Voronoi percolation in the plane. In the first model, the Voronoi tiling does not evolve in time while the colors of the cells are resampled at rate 1. In the second model, the centers of the cells move according to (independent) long range stable L\\'evy processes but the colors do not evolve in time. We prove that for these two dynamical processes there exist almost surely exceptional times with an unbounded monochromatic component.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Annals of Probability (https://www.imstat.org/publications/aop/aop_49_3/aop_49_3.pdf)\nciteturn4search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 5.4 years ago; THE ANNEALED SPECTRAL SAMPLE OF VORONOI PERCOLATION ... In this paper, we introduce and study the annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation, which is a continuous and finite point process in R² whose definition is mostly inspired by the spectral sample of Bernoulli percolation introduced in (Acta Math. 205 (2010) 19–104) by Garban, Pete and Schramm.\nThe Annals of Probability  \n2021, Vol. 49, No. 3, 1554–1606  \nhttps://doi.org/10.1214/20-AOP1494  \n© Institute of Mathematical Statistics, 2021  \n\nTHE ANNEALED SPECTRAL SAMPLE OF VORONOI PERCOLATION\n\nBY HUGO VANNEUVILLE\n\nICJ, Université Lyon 1, vanneuville@math.univ-lyon1.fr\n\nIn this paper, we introduce and study the annealed spectral sample of Voronoi percolation, which is a continuous and finite point process in R² whose definition is mostly inspired by the spectral sample of Bernoulli percolation introduced in (Acta Math. 205 (2010) 19–104) by Garban, Pete and Schramm. We show a clustering effect as well as estimates on the full lower tail of this spectral object.\n\nOur main motivation is the study of two models of dynamical critical Voronoi percolation in the plane. In the first model, the Voronoi tiling does not evolve in time while the colors of the cells are resampled at rate 1. In the second model, the centers of the cells move according to (independent) long range stable Lévy processes but the colors do not evolve in time. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVol. 49, No. 3, May 2021 of The Annals of Probability | JSTOR (https://www.jstor.org/stable/e27172536)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: last week;      * THE ANNEALED SPECTRAL SAMPLE OF VORONOI PERCOLATION\n\nA NONAMENABLE “FACTOR” OF A EUCLIDEAN SPACE (pp. 1427-1449)\n\nÁdám Timár\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/27172549\n\n     * ADDITIVE FUNCTIONALS AS ROUGH PATHS\n\nADDITIVE FUNCTIONALS AS ROUGH PATHS (pp. 1450-1479)\n\nJean-Dominique Deuschel, Tal Orenshtein and Nicolas Perkowski\n\nhttps://www.jstor.or